First, `is_dedicated_test_file`:

```python
def is_dedicated_test_file(path: str) -> bool:
    """A file whose whole contents are tests.

    In these files a bare `.unwrap()` / `.expect(` counts as an oracle. Everywhere else
    it does not, because production code is full of both and their removal is not a
    weakened test -- that would flood the denominator with noise from `src/`.

    A `tests/` PATH SEGMENT, not only a leading `tests/`. The bug this fixes (day 201,
    #932) was measured with a control pair: one identical hunk body, two path shapes,
    `--stdin`. `tests/x.rs` reported `skipped, vocabulary could not read: 1` and
    `tokio/tests/x.rs` reported NOTHING -- because a cargo WORKSPACE keeps integration
    tests at `<crate>/tests/*.rs`, so the leading-prefix test was false for every one of
    them. On tokio-rs/tokio, 244 test files sit at `*/tests/*.rs` and 320 window hunks
    touch a path containing `/tests/`, so the whole subject was read as "no test files
    here" -- and the failure direction is the bad one, because "could not look" was
    rendered as "looked; clean", the exact confusion this counter's own docstring
    refuses. `_tests.rs` is kept as a second clause: it is the other house convention for
    a tests module (`src/main_tests.rs`).

    The segment test is `parts[:-1]`, so the LAST component can never satisfy it: a
    file literally named `tests` (or `src/tests.rs`) is not, by name alone, a dedicated
    test file. `tests/x.rs` and `tokio/tests/x.rs` both match; `src/contests/x.rs`,
    `src/latest.rs` and `src/tests.rs` do not.

    WHAT THIS DOES NOT LICENSE is written at the counter (see the
    SKIPPED_UNKNOWN_VOCABULARY note above): the day-200 ripgrep and day-201 tokio rows
    were taken with the narrower predicate and are append-only records of what the
    instrument SAID, not of what was there.
    """
    return "tests" in path.split("/")[:-1] or path.endswith("_tests.rs")
```

